      "command": "Explain",
      "modelAnswer": "The heart is a double pump that moves blood by mass transport, which is needed because diffusion alone is too slow over the distances in a large animal. The right side receives deoxygenated blood from the vena cava into the right atrium and pumps it from the right ventricle through the pulmonary artery to the lungs. The left side receives oxygenated blood from the pulmonary veins into the left atrium and pumps it from the left ventricle through the aorta to the body. A septum separates the two sides so oxygenated and deoxygenated blood do not mix. Blood passes through the heart twice in each complete circulation of the body: the pulmonary circuit runs at lower pressure, which protects the delicate lung capillaries, while the systemic circuit runs at high pressure to deliver blood quickly to all tissues. The atria have thin walls because they pump blood only into the ventricles. The ventricles have thicker muscular walls, and the left ventricle is thickest because it must generate the highest pressure to push blood around the whole body. The heart muscle itself is supplied by the coronary arteries. The cardiac cycle has three stages. In atrial systole the atria contract, raising atrial pressure above ventricular pressure, so blood is forced through the open atrioventricular (tricuspid and bicuspid) valves to fill the ventricles. In ventricular systole the ventricles contract; ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure, closing the atrioventricular valves and preventing backflow into the atria. When ventricular pressure exceeds the pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery, the semilunar valves open and blood is ejected into the arteries. In cardiac diastole the heart muscle relaxes; ventricular pressure falls below arterial pressure, so the semilunar valves close and prevent blood flowing back into the ventricles. Blood returning from the veins fills the atria and, once atrial pressure exceeds ventricular pressure, passes into the ventricles. Valves open and close only in response to these pressure differences, so blood always flows in one direction. The thick, elastic walls of the arteries stretch when blood is ejected and recoil during diastole, maintaining pressure and smoothing flow, while veins have valves to prevent backflow at low pressure. Together, the separation of the two circuits, the different wall thicknesses and the pressure-driven valves ensure one-way, efficient circulation at the pressure each circuit needs."
