      "command": "Explain",
      "modelAnswer": "Genetic screening tests individuals for the presence of specific alleles associated with disease. Methods include PCR amplification of the relevant gene followed by analysis (DNA sequencing, allele-specific probes, restriction analysis), and increasingly whole-genome or exome sequencing. Pre-implantation genetic diagnosis (PGD) tests embryos created by IVF before implantation; prenatal screening tests foetuses (chorionic villus sampling, amniocentesis or non-invasive analysis of cell-free foetal DNA in maternal blood); newborn screening (the heel-prick test) detects treatable conditions such as PKU and CF; carrier screening tests adults considering reproduction; predictive testing identifies risk for conditions such as Huntington's disease or BRCA-related cancers. Benefits: early detection allows treatment (PKU, CF), informed reproductive choices (carrier or prenatal screening), preventive measures (mastectomy or surveillance for BRCA carriers), and access to support. Ethical issues are wide-ranging. Autonomy and consent: testing must be informed and voluntary, and individuals have a right not to know. Confidentiality: genetic results affect family members, who may not have consented; insurance discrimination and employment discrimination are concerns (some jurisdictions have legislated against them). Termination: prenatal screening for some conditions raises concerns about disability rights and the message sent about the value of disabled lives. PGD raises eugenics concerns, especially for non-medical traits. Predictive testing for untreatable conditions like Huntington's carries severe psychological consequences and may affect family planning, employment and relationships. Variants of uncertain significance complicate interpretation and counselling. Equity of access is a global issue: most testing is only available in wealthy countries or to wealthy patients. Robust ethical frameworks, professional counselling, regulation and ongoing public dialogue are essential as the technology rapidly expands. Used responsibly, genetic screening can prevent suffering and save lives, but the social context determines whether it is empowering or harmful."
